Cholangitis
Gastrointestinal & Hepatology
RUQ pain, Fever with rigors, Jaundice, Dilated common bile duct

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GIH-008
Reason for Visit: Sudden onset of uncontrollable shivering, high fever, severe right-sided abdominal pain, and yellowing of the eyes.
Location: Private residence.
Present at Visit: Patient, spouse.
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage revealed a textbook presentation of Charcot's triad (fever, jaundice, RUQ pain), indicating acute biliary sepsis. This is a life-threatening medical emergency. Urgent after-hours physical assessment was mandated to confirm clinical signs of severe sepsis and immediately facilitate emergency ambulance transfer for intravenous antibiotics and urgent biliary decompression before the onset of septic shock.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I'm freezing and shaking uncontrollably, my side is in agony, and my husband says my eyes have turned yellow."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a sudden onset of severe right upper quadrant pain beginning 6 hours ago. Pain is sharp and continuous. Accompanied by severe nausea and recurrent vomiting. Developed swinging high fevers with intense rigors (shaking chills) 2 hours ago. Spouse noted scleral icterus (yellowing of eyes) and dark urine today. Patient reports a history of previously diagnosed gallstones but no prior surgeries. Denies altered mental status or profound dizziness currently.
Functional Status: Bedbound, actively shivering, unable to walk unassisted.
Social/Safety Concerns: Rapid clinical deterioration; highly dependent on spouse for current management.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 100/60 | HR: 125 | Temp: 39.5 | RR: 24 | SpO2: 95% | Wt: 80 Kg
General: Patient is acutely unwell, toxic-appearing, diaphoretic, and experiencing active rigors.
Physical Exam: Marked scleral icterus and visible jaundice of the skin. Abdominal examination reveals localized severe right upper quadrant tenderness with guarding and a palpable, tender liver edge. Tachycardia and tachypnoea present, indicating severe systemic sepsis. Clinical presentation definitively demonstrates RUQ pain, fever with rigors, and jaundice; dilated common bile duct is presumed pending urgent imaging.
Environmental Observations: Emesis basin at bedside containing bilious fluid.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular medications. Took paracetamol 2 hours ago with no reduction in fever.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Cholangitis - Status: Exacerbated (Septic)
Differentials:

Acute Cholecystitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of frank jaundice and dark urine, which indicate complete biliary tree obstruction rather than isolated gallbladder inflammation.

Acute Hepatitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The sudden, hyperacute onset of high fevers with intense rigors and severe, localized pain, which are atypical for viral or autoimmune hepatitis.

Pyelonephritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of clinical jaundice and predominantly anterior RUQ pain rather than isolated costovertebral angle tenderness and dysuria.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low (Medical risk is critically high)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an unequivocal clinical picture of acute ascending cholangitis, characterized by the classic Charcot's triad of severe right upper quadrant pain, high fever with rigors, and clinical jaundice. The objective presence of significant tachycardia, tachypnoea, and borderline hypotension firmly indicates advancing biliary sepsis. This condition carries a high mortality rate if left untreated. An urgent out-of-hours home visit was absolutely necessary to definitively recognize the septic state, bypass routine primary care delays, and initiate immediate emergency medical transport for life-saving intravenous antibiotics and urgent endoscopic retrograde cholangiopancreatography (ERCP).

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Immediately contacted emergency services (000) for priority ambulance dispatch. Administered IM Ceftriaxone 1g (as per local emergency rural/home-visit protocols for acute sepsis pending transport) and Ondansetron 4mg IM.
Positive Behavioral Change: Remain completely flat in bed while awaiting the ambulance. Keeping your head low physically maintains blood flow to your brain and major organs, preventing a dangerous drop in blood pressure caused by the severe infection.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Acute hospital admission required; GP follow-up post-discharge.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Patient is currently awaiting ambulance transport. If patient loses consciousness, becomes acutely confused, or stops breathing before paramedics arrive, spouse must commence basic life support if trained and update 000 dispatch.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: This is a life-threatening emergency requiring immediate hospital intervention.
Clinical Course: Without urgent intravenous antibiotics and a procedure to unblock the bile duct, this infection will rapidly cause septic shock and multi-organ failure.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not attempt to drive the patient to the hospital; wait for the ambulance. Paramedics can provide life-saving intravenous fluids and blood pressure support en route if required.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A gallstone has completely blocked the main drainage tube from your liver, causing stagnant bile to become severely infected. The bacteria are now entering your bloodstream, causing the severe shaking and fevers. You need urgent hospital care to kill the bacteria and mechanically remove the blockage.
Actionable Steps: Do not eat or drink anything. The surgical team will need your stomach to be completely empty to safely perform the procedure to remove the blockage.

